  45: ## T2 — Server attempt and sender fencing with key correlation
  46: Reasoning: Every outbox guarantee rests on a server contract that does not exist yet; it must be proven at 
integration level, including old-client compatibility, before any client queue relies on it.
  47: Task: Keyed creates may carry an attempt generation and an expected sender; with them, a create commits only 
when its generation is current for (sender, conversation, key) at commit time, and a sender mismatch is rejected 
without storing. A new fence/lookup route, for the caller's own key only, atomically returns the stored message or 
makes every lower generation unable to commit, rejects older generations, never creates a message, and rejects anon 
and other users' keys. The original submitted composition is stored for replay comparison. Create and fence responses 
carry `clientMessageId`; outcome codes follow BR-005. Compatibility: unkeyed creates behave as today; keyed requests 
without a generation (Phase 4 client) keep the Phase 4 keyed contract for text, attachment, and voice, including 
replay and 409, and commit only if no fence state exists for the key at commit time (Standing rules, D4); otherwise 
they store nothing and are rejected as stale.
  48: Done when: AC-044 (server part), AC-045 cases (a)–(c), and the AC-046 status codes hold against local Postgres 
and the real routes with at most one stored message per key, and Phase 4 keyed and legacy unkeyed requests still 
behave as before.
  49: Verify by: New messaging DB integration tests for AC-044 (crafted mismatched sender, anon fence, other-user 
key), AC-045 (row-lock-blocked insert, held stale request, stale fence), AC-046 codes, generation-less requests losing 
to a fence (held request and row-lock-blocked insert, nothing stored afterwards), and compatibility (unkeyed text; 
Phase 4 keyed text, attachment, voice; replays of their stored messages and 409) pass; RLS review gate recorded.
